<!--
  The application icon, compiled into the binary.

  A resource rather than a file read from disk at start-up: the icon has to be
  there whether SurView is running from an installed prefix, from an AppImage's
  own mount point, or straight out of a developer's build tree, and a path
  relative to the executable is a different path in each of those. The packaged
  copy in share/icons is what the DESKTOP needs; this is what the window needs.

  IT IS THE PNG, NOT THE SVG. Decoding SVG needs Qt's own SVG image format
  plugin, which is not part of qt6-base: on a machine carrying only the base
  package the icon renders to nothing and the window silently has none. Found by
  CI, which is exactly that machine, against a developer box where the plugin
  happened to be installed and every test passed. PNG is decoded by Qt itself,
  everywhere, with nothing else installed. The SVG is still what is INSTALLED
  for the desktop entry, where the desktop environment renders and scales it.

  packaging/surview-256.png is rendered from packaging/surview.svg, which stays
  the one source of the mark. tools/make-icon.sh runs the renderer; the command
  is there rather than here because an XML comment cannot contain two dashes in
  a row, and every one of its options begins with them.
-->
<RCC>
  <qresource prefix="/">
    <file alias="surview.png">../packaging/surview-256.png</file>
  </qresource>
</RCC>
